  "live_records": {
    "q-38612": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38612",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$  such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ 2x-8y \\\\ 6x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=2 \\\\ 2x-8y=8 \\\\ 6x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =2 \\\\ 4(4+4y)-2y & =2 \\\\ 14y & =-14 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+10y & =k \\\\ 6(0)+10(-1) & =k \\\\ k & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-10.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
