    },
    "q-80818": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80818",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=9 \\\\ x+2y=8 \\\\ -2x+7y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=8-2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =9 \\\\ 3(8-2y)+y & =9 \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-2y \\\\  & =8-2(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =k \\\\ -2(2)+7(3) & =k \\\\ k & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=17.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
