    "q-80824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80824",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$  such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y \\\\ -3x+9y \\\\ 4x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y=12 \\\\ -3x+9y=15 \\\\ 4x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=3y-5$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =12 \\\\ -2(3y-5)+7y & =12 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y-5 \\\\  & =3(2)-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5y & =k \\\\ 4(1)-5(2) & =k \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
