    "q-81241": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81241",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -3x+6y \\\\ 2x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -3x+6y \\\\ 2x+5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=k \\\\ -3x+6y=-3 \\\\ 2x+5y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=2y+1$, which we substitute into the third equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =20 \\\\ 2(2y+1)+5y & =20 \\\\ 9y & =18 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y+1 \\\\  & =2(2)+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the first equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =k \\\\ 5+2 & =k \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
